{pause up}
## What is a capability?

{.definition}
An **unforgeable token** that both **designates** a resource and
**grants the authority** to use it — no reference => no access.
<!--alt: A **communicable**, **unforgeable** token of authority -->


{pause}
Unix already has one: the **file descriptor**. An int that [designates]{.em}
an open file and lets you act on it — you cannot [forge]{.em} one, the
kernel hands it to you.

{pause}
OCaml too with **channels**: `in_channel` / `out_channel` (refined read/write capabilities).

{pause}
Capabilities aren't a new idea — Unix and OCaml already use them.
This talk just pushes the [discipline]{.em} further.

{pause up}
## Capabilities are abstract types

{.example title="Cap.mli"}
```ocaml
module Process : sig
  type fork   (* abstract: can not be forged *)
  type exit
  type chdir
  ...
end

type fork = < fork : Process.fork >
```

{pause up}
## Combining capabilities

Capabilities are deliberately fine-grained — one per resource. Real
code needs many at once, so we need a way to combine them.

{pause}
Object types [naturally]{.em} fit: row-typed, they combine for free —
no OCaml extension needed.

{pause}
{.example title="Cap.mli"}
```ocaml
type network = < network : Network.t >

(* combine capabilities just by listing them *)
type fork_and_network = < fork; network >
```

{pause up}
## The only door in: `Cap.main`

{.example}
```ocaml
type all_caps =
  < fork; network; random; stdout; stdin; exit; chdir; ... >

val main : (all_caps -> 'a) -> 'a
```

{pause}
- Callable exactly once — checked dynamically. {pause}
- Hands your entry point **every** capability. {pause}
- The entry point then **downcasts** (`:>`) to give each callee only
  the subset it needs.

{pause up}
## Capability-aware wrappers

`CapUnix`, `CapSys`, `CapStdlib`, `CapRandom`, ... wrap the standard
library one dangerous function at a time. All three pieces, together:

{pause}
{style="display:flex; gap:20px; align-items:flex-start"}
> {.example title="Cap.mli" style="flex:1"}
> ```ocaml
> type fork = < fork : Process.fork >
> type wait = < wait : Process.wait >
> ```
>
> {pause}
> {.example title="CapUnix.ml" style="flex:1"}
> ```ocaml
> let fork _caps = Unix.fork
> let wait _caps = Unix.wait
> ```
>
> {pause}
> {.example title="mymodule.ml" style="flex:1"}
> ```ocaml
> let restart
>     (caps : < Cap.fork; Cap.wait; .. >) () =
>   match CapUnix.fork caps () with
>   | 0 -> exec_new_version ()
>   | _ -> ignore (CapUnix.wait caps ())
> ```

{pause}
The `.mli` signature does all the enforcement — the implementation
doesn't even need to touch `caps`.

{pause up}
# Capability rules

`Cap.ml` alone doesn't stop anyone from calling `Unix.fork` directly
instead of `CapUnix.fork`.

{pause}
Enforce it with a Semgrep **capability rule**:

{pause}
{.example}
```json
{
  id: 'use-caps',
  match: { any: ['Unix.fork', 'Unix.wait', ...] },
  languages: ['ocaml'],
  severity: 'ERROR',
  message: |||
    Do not use Sys.xxx or Unix.xxx directly.
    Use CapSys or CapUnix and pass the required capability.
  |||,
}
```

{pause up}
## Going one step further

{.example}
```ocaml
(* TCB.ml, shadows Stdlib *)
let open_in = ()
let open_out = ()
let exit = ()
module Obj = struct end   (* no Obj.magic *)
```

{pause}
`-open TCB` still opens `Stdlib` underneath, so `TCB.ml` reuses the
same names and retypes them to `()` — killing the dangerous bindings
outright, everywhere, for free.

{pause up}
# Experience report

Rolled out on three real codebases:

{pause}
- **osemgrep** — the OCaml core of Semgrep itself {pause}
- the **mmm** browser {pause}
- several **Plan 9** systems programs ported to OCaml in **XiX**: a
  build system, a shell, a windowing system, a toolchain {pause}

{pause}
Each time: a handful of capability annotations plus a handful of
Semgrep rules was enough to enforce capabilities across the whole
codebase.

{pause up}
## A concrete win

{.block title="Found deep in the call stack"}
While migrating Semgrep, the capability rules flagged a call to the
external `uname` program via `Unix.open_process_in` — added by a
junior developer, missed in code review, and a performance
bottleneck.

{pause up}
## Why reviewers like it

Once a codebase is migrated, a quick-and-dirty hack needs a new
capability threaded through the **whole call chain** — much harder to
sneak past review.

{pause up}
## And for AI coding agents

{pause}
- They pick up the capability discipline instantly, no coaching
  needed. {pause}
- A reviewer can trust the *interface*: if it needs no dangerous
  capability, the type system guarantees the implementation can't use
  one either. {pause}
- Stronger types become a **sandbox** around generated code, not just
  documentation.

{pause up}
# FAQ

Practical compromises — the "less radical than Haskell" part.

{pause}
{.block title="Doesn't Logs need a capability too?"}
No — [ambient authority]{.em}, on purpose. Threading a capability
through every log call would drown signatures in noise for little
security benefit.

{pause}
{.block title="What about time, profiling?"}
Same call: ambient authority, low risk, not worth the friction.

{pause}
{.block title="Won't every signature explode in size?"}
Row polymorphism (`..`) — a function only names what it actually
uses; the rest stays polymorphic.

{pause}
{.block title="Why object types, not records?"}
A record needs an *exact* field match. An object's open row (`..`)
accepts anything with *at least* these fields — so a bigger
capability set is automatically a [subtype]{.em} of a smaller one, no
boilerplate.

{pause}
{.block title="Isn't the space of possible capabilities huge?"}
Not really — real systems have surprisingly few core syscalls. Plan 9
has just **39** :) (`fork`, `exec`, `wait`, `read`, `write`, `brk`,
`alarm`, etc.)

{pause}
{.block title="What stops UUnix / UStdlib escape hatches?"}
They exist only for migration — greppable, rare, and they stand out
in code review.

{pause up}
# Related work

{pause}
- **eio** (OCaml) — the direct inspiration: effects tracked via
  capabilities on I/O. {pause}
- **cap-std** (Rust) — capability-safe wrappers around the OS,
  removing ambient authority. {pause}
- **Pony** — reference capabilities baked into the type system
  itself. {pause}
- **Haskell** — mtl-style typeclasses (`MonadFileSystem`, ...) or
  effect libraries (`polysemy`, `fused-effects`, `effectful`) can get
  similar fine-grained tracking, at the cost of extra machinery on
  top of plain `IO`.

{pause up}
